Accession Number   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Report Status  CorrectedType  Surgical Pathology Pathology Report   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  OPERATION DATE  17 REMOVED_MONTH 03 ACCESSIONED ON  18 REMOVED_MONTH 03 at 09 17 CLINICAL DATA  History of invasive ductal carcinoma in  REMOVED_CASE_ID   grade III III  with dermal and epidermal involvement                                                                                      FINAL DIAGNOSIS     BREAST  LEFT   SIMPLE MASTECTOMY  ANGIOSARCOMA  HIGH GRADE  GRADE REMOVED_DATE OF 3   EXTENDING THROUGHOUT THE ENTIRE SPECIMEN  17 CM   AND BROADLY PRESENT AT THE POSTERIOR AND SKIN MARGINS  INVASIVE DUCTAL CARCINOMA  GRADE REMOVED_DATE OF 3  PRESENT WITHIN AN AREA OF DENSE SCLEROSIS CONSISTENT WITH SITE OF TREATED TUMOR  AND EXTENDING TO THE POSTERIOR MARGIN  ONE LYMPH NODE WITH NO EVIDENCE OF MALIGNANCY  SEE NOTE  NOTE  The angiosarcoma may have arisen from the invasive ductal carcinoma and thus represent a component of metaplastic carcinoma  or it may possibly be a postirradiation sarcoma  Although the angiosarcoma and invasive ductal carcinoma are juxtaposed in several areas  clear merging of the two tumor types is not identified  The angiosarcoma diffusely involves the skin and focally invades the deep skeletal muscle  The angiosarcoma tumor cells are diffusely positive for factor VIII   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   and vimentin  and are focally positive for  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   They are negative for cytokeratins  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  3 Cam5 2  wide spectrum  and  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   as well as S 100  desmin  and ulex  These immunohistochemical findings support the diagnosis of angiosarcoma  Slides of the patient s prior biopsy   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   are not currently available for comparison  Receptor studies will be reported in an addendum  Diagnosis by  MELINDA J  FAN  MD Signed On  26 REMOVED_MONTH 03 at 11 52                                                                                     IMMUNOHISTOCHEMISTRY Addendum     RESULTS OF IMMUNOHISTOCHEMICAL STAINING FOR RECEPTOR PROTEINS Breast  Left  block C   The invasive ductal carcinoma tumor cells stain for estrogen receptor protein  The invasive ductal carcinoma tumor cells do not stain for progesterone receptor protein  The invasive ductal carcinoma tumor cells do not show overexpression of HER 2 c erbB 2  Breast  Left  block J   Rare angiosarcoma tumor cells stain faintly for estrogen receptor protein  Rare angiosarcoma tumor cells stain faintly for progesterone receptor protein  The angiosarcoma tumor cells do not show overexpression of HER 2 c erbB 2  See note  Note  These tests were developed and their performance characteristics determined by the Pathology Department at the Massachusetts General Hospital  They have not been cleared or approved by the U S  Food and Drug Administration  FDA   The FDA has determined that such clearance or approval is not necessary  this laboratory has established and verified the test s accuracy and precision  Added by  Atul Kumar Bhan  M D  Signed On  02 REMOVED_MONTH 03 at 16 28     IMMUNOHISTOCHEMISTRY Addendum     THIS ADDENDUM HAS BEEN DELETED FOR THIS REPORT GROSS DESCRIPTION  Received fresh   labeled REMOVE_PATIENT_NAME  patient s name  unit number  and  left breast   is a 17 x 14 x 9cm simple mastectomy specimen  The entire anterior surface is covered by skin  which is notable for numerous fungating  necrotic tan red and green brown polypoid masses which cover nearly 50  of the surface  The radial skin resection margins are grossly positive for tumor in several areas  The posterior resection margin consists of adipose tissue and skeletal tissue  The resection margins  all posterior  are inked black  and the specimen is serially sectioned  The dermis and underlying parenchyma is diffusely thickened by tumor throughout the entire breast  Deeper in the tissue is a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 2 0cm firm fibrotic region that grossly abuts the attached skeletal muscles  Additional nodules of tumor are scattered throughout the breast parenchyma  Tumor is grossly present at the deep margin  Representative sections are submitted as follows  A D  Fibrotic mass to deep resection margin  E F  Skin  G H  Other masses in parenchyma  I J  Skin margins  en face  K  Tumor to skeletal muscle  L  Tumor to deep margin  Also received in this case  is a nonoriented piece of lobulated yellow grossly hemorrhagic adipose tissue measuring REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 2 0cm  The external surface is entirely inked black and the specimen is serially sectioned to reveal a heterogeneous brown red white ill defined cut surface  A representative section is submitted in cassette K  Resident  JOSHUA B  KISH  MD                                                                                 SOURCE CARE UNIT  MGH INPATIENT  FLR UNKNOWN  REPORTS TO  Cameron Dorrans Wright  M D  William Gerald Austen  M D  PART   BREAST LEFT  SIMPLE MASTECTOMY , Checked.
