Accession Number   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Report Status  Final Type  Surgical Pathology Pathology Report   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  OPERATION DATE  17 REMOVED_MONTH 95 ACCESSIONED ON  17 REMOVED_MONTH 95 at 13 23 CLINICAL DATA  The patient is a 77 year old female with a left breast mass  biopsied on REMOVED_DATE  diagnosed as infiltrating ductal carcinoma grade REMOVED_DATE                                                                                     FINAL DIAGNOSIS     BREAST  LEFT   MASTECTOMY    NEGATIVE FOR RESIDUAL CARCINOMA  ELEVEN AXILLARY LYMPH NODES ARE FREE OF CARCINOMA    BIOPSY SITES WITH FIBROSIS  GRANULATION TISSUE  CHRONIC INFLAMMATION  HISTIOCYTIC AND FOREIGN BODY GIANT CELL REACTION TO SUTURE MATERIAL  Diagnosis by  Michael Pins  M D  Signed On  23 REMOVED_MONTH 95 at 15 08                                                                                 GROSS DESCRIPTION  Received fresh   labeled REMOVE_PATIENT_NAME  patient s name  unit number  and  Left breast   is a 810 gram  REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 3 3cm unoriented left mastectomy specimen  which includes an REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 1 5cm left axillary tail  and which is partially covered by a REMOVED_DATE x 6 0cm skin ellipse  The skin ellipse includes a REMOVED_DATE x 3 0cm areola with a 2 0cm in length well healed transverse scar  3 0cm inferior to the nipple  and another 2 0cm in length well healed transverse scar  1 5cm superomedial to the nipple  The remainder of the skin is diffusely tan white  and otherwise unremarkable  The deep resection margin is a smooth plane  The specimen is inked and sectioned to reveal a prior REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 1 5cm cystic biopsy cavity lined by red tan  grumose material  and surrounded by a rim of intense fibrosis and semifirm sulphur yellow discoloration  The biopsy site is immediately subjacent to the epidermal scar located 3 0cm inferior to the nipple  and approaches to within 0 3cm of the inferior margin  2 2cm of the deep inked margin  4 0cm of the medial margin  6 0cm of the superior margin  and 9 0cm of the lateral margin  Additional sectioning of the breast parenchyma reveals a second prior REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 0 4cm biopsy site consisting of intense stellate fibrosis and semifirm sulphur yellow discoloration  The biopsy site is immediately subjacent to the second epidermal scar located 1 5cm superomedial to the nipple  and approaches to within 1 0cm of the superior margin  2 5cm of the medial margin  2 5cm of the deep inked margin  5 3cm of the inferior margin  and 12 0cm of the lateral margin  A third REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 0 4cm area of stellate fibrosis and sulphur yellow discoloration is located midway between the two biopsy sites  2 0cm deep to the nipple  and approaching to within 1 7cm of the superior margin  2 0cm of the deep inked margin  3 0cm of the medial margin  7 0cm of the inferior margin  and 11 5cm of the lateral margin  The remainder of the breast parenchyma consists of mature  lobular  yellow  adipose tissue with interculating fibrous bands  The axillary tail is explored for lymph nodes  eleven lymph nodes are identified in the upper one third of the tail  SECTION CODE  A E  inferior margin and skin  inferior biopsy   F G  superior margin and skin  superomedial biopsy   H  superior margin  fibrous area   I  deep inked margin  inferior biopsy   J  deep inked margin  superomedial biopsy   K  deep inked margin  fibrous area   L  medial margin  en face  M  lateral margin  en face  N  nipple  O T  lymph nodes  Resident  JAMES J  TIESINGA  MD                                                                                 SOURCE CARE UNIT  MGH INPATIENT  FLR UNKNOWN  REPORTS TO  Peter Hedberg  M D  PART   BREAST  LEFT    report_end  , Unchecked.
Accession Number   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Report Status  Updated Type  Surgical Pathology Pathology Report  MS 08  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  CASE  MS 08  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME  Date Taken  REMOVED_DATE Source Care Unit  Same Day Surgery  WACC 3 Path Subspecialty Service  Breast 1 Results To  MICHELE ANNETTE GADD  M D  Resident  Anna McDonald  M D  Signed Out by  Ronald Balassanian  M D             Revised Report            Reason for Amendment  1  Corrected Final Diagnosis Reason for Addendum  1  Additional Immunohistochemistry Results Reason for Amendment  2  Corrected Transcription Error not in Final Diagnosis CLINICAL DATA  The patient is a 60 year old woman with left breast enhancement seen on MRI  A prior core biopsy demonstrated probable lobular neoplasia  FINAL DIAGNOSIS  BREAST  LEFT   EXCISION  1  DUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU  2  EXTENSIVE LOBULAR NEOPLASIA  LOBULAR CARCINOMA IN SITU  WITH CALCIFICATIONS   SEE TABLE 1 AND NOTE   TABLE OF PATHOLOGICAL FINDINGS  1 DUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU  DCIS  TUMOR SIZE  Involving 4 of 6 blocks  GRADE  3  CALCIFICATIONS  Limited to the carcinoma in situ  Both LCIS and DCIS   MARGIN OF DUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU  Positive along the inked margin of the specimen along a broad front  STAINS FOR RECEPTORS  Requested on block  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   NOTE  Sections of the excision sample reveal large ducts distended by tumor cells  In many areas the tumor cells are large and pleomorphic with abundant cytoplasm and occasional single mucin vacuoles  A few cells have a signet ring appearance  The nuclei are mildly enlarged with mildly irregular contours and granular chromatin  The morphologic findings in these areas are compatible with pleomorphic variant of lobular carcinoma in situ  Intermixed with these areas are other ducts with cells that have a similar appearance but with a distinctly cribriform pattern compatible with a component of DCIS  An immunohistochemical stain for E cadherin on Slide  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  is largely negative and supports the diagnosis of LCIS  however  there is weak membrane staining in the ductal cells in the areas of cribriform architecture supporting a diagnosis DCIS  Stains for p63  myosin  calponin and keratin performed on slides  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   and  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  do not show evidence of an invasive carcinoma  Overall  the morphologic findings are compatible with carcinoma in situ with both ductal and lobular features  Immunostains for estrogen receptor and progesterone receptor will be reported in an addendum  This case was amended to correct the final diagnosis  On initial review  the findings were interpreted as extensive LCIS  However  upon review of the e cadherin as well as the ER  PR and Her 2neu stain the findings were interpreted as both ductal and lobular carcinoma in situ  The final diagnosis and note in the report has been revised to reflect these changes  By his her signature below  the pathologist listed as making the Final Diagnosis certifies that he she has personally reviewed this case and confirmed or corrected the diagnoses  GROSS DESCRIPTION  Received fresh   labeled REMOVE_PATIENT_NAME  Donovan  REMOVE_MRN and  left breast needle loc   is an unoriented portion of fibroadipose tissue  5 0 x REMOVED_DATE x 0 6   with two needle localization wires in place  The specimen is entirely inked black and serially sectioned to reveal no mass lesions  The specimen is entirely included in  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  with   prior biopsy site in  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   ADDENDUM NOTE  REASON  1 QUANTITATIVE RESULTS OF IMMUNOHISTOCHEMICAL STAINING FOR RECEPTOR PROTEINS Breast  Left  block  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    The ductal carcinoma in situ tumor cells do not stain for estrogen receptor protein  The ductal carcinoma in situ tumor cells do not stain for progesterone receptor protein  The ductal carcinoma in situ tumor cells show strong  3   overexpression of HER 2 c erbB 2  See note  Note  Estrogen and progesterone receptor quantitation  The percent of immunoreactive nuclei in the tumor cells is assessed visually and reported as follows  The tumor cells stain    10  of nuclei stained  Some tumor cells stain  1 to  10  of nuclei stained  Rare tumor cells stain    1  of nuclei stained  The tumor cells do not stain  0  of nuclei stained  HER 2 c erbB 2 quantitation  the tumor cells membrane immunoreactivity is evaluated visually and reported as follows  Do not show overexpression  0 to 1   no minimal immunoreactivity or incomplete membrane staining of tumor cells  Show moderate  2   overexpression  weak to moderate complete membrane immunoreactivity in   10  of tumor cells  Show strong  3   overexpression  strong complete membrane immunoreactivity in   10  of tumor cells  These tests were developed and their performance characteristics determined by the Pathology Department at the Massachusetts General Hospital  They have not been cleared or approved by the U S  Food and Drug Administration  FDA   The FDA has determined that such clearance or approval is not necessary  this laboratory has established and verified the test s accuracy and precision  Amended report This report was amended to correct the acronym in the title of the table of pathologic findings  The acronym previously listed as  LCIS  has been changed to  DCIS   Final Diagnosis by Ronald Balassanian M D   on REMOVED_DATE Amendment  1 by Ronald Balassanian M D   on REMOVED_DATE Addendum  1 by Atul K Bhan M D   on REMOVED_DATE Amendment  2 by Ronald Balassanian M D   Electronically signed on REMOVED_DATE PART   BREAST  LEFT   EXCISION  report_end  , Unchecked.
Accession Number   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Report Status  Final Type  Surgical Pathology Pathology Report   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  OPERATION DATE  27 REMOVED_MONTH 98 ACCESSIONED ON  27 REMOVED_MONTH 98 at 10 29 CLINICAL DATA  Left breast cancer                                                                                      FINAL DIAGNOSIS     BREAST  LEFT   MASTECTOMY  1  INVASIVE DUCTAL CARCINOMA  REMOVED_DATE CM  POORLY DIFFERENTIATED   SEE NOTE   2  DUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU  HIGH GRADE  MULTIFOCAL  PRESENT ASSOCIATED WITH AND EXTENDING AWAY FROM THE INVASIVE COMPONENT  AND PRESENT REMOVED_DATE CM FROM THE SUPERIOR AND INFERIOR RESECTION MARGINS  3  METASTATIC DUCTAL CARCINOMA TO 1 OF 13 LYMPH NODES  NOTE  Definitive blood vascular invasion is not seen  The margins of resection appear free of invasive carcinoma  SOFT TISSUE  ADDITIONAL LEFT AXILLARY TISSUE   ONE LYMPH NODE FREE OF CARCINOMA  BREAST  RIGHT   REDUCTION  FIBROCYSTIC CHANGES  THERE IS NO EVIDENCE OF MALIGNANCY  Diagnosis by  Dennis Sgroi  M D  Signed On  01 MAY 98 at 11 23                                                                                     RECEPTOR IMMUNOHISTOCHEMISTRY Addendum     RESULTS OF STAINING OF FIXED TISSUE FOR HORMONE RECEPTOR PROTEINS BREAST  LEFT   The tumor cells do not stain for estrogen receptor protein  The tumor cells do not stain for progesterone receptor protein  See note  Note  In this laboratory  we used this antibody to stain 100 breast carcinomas prepared in the usual way  paraffin sections  and we compared the results with determinations of estrogen receptor by biochemical assay  91  of tumors that stained with this antibody were positive for estrogen receptors by biochemical assay  96  of tumors that failed to stain with this antibody were negative for estrogen receptors by biochemical assay  When we compared the results of staining of paraffin sections with the results of staining of frozen sections  the predictive value of a positive result was 97  and the predictive value of a negative result was 95   In this laboratory  we also compared the results of staining of 100 routinely processed breast carcinomas with determinations of progesterone receptors by a hormone binding assay  Sixty five percent of tumors that stained with this antibody contained progesterone receptors by the biochemical assay  and 98  of tumors that failed to stain lacked the receptors  Added by  Frederick Carl Koerner  M D  Signed On  13 MAY 98 at 16 56     HER 2 Addendum     RESULTS OF IMMUNOHISTOCHEMICAL STAINING FOR HER 2 c erb REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  BREAST  LEFT   The tumor cells show strong overexpression of HER 2 c erb 2  See note  Note  This test was developed and its performance characteristics determined by the Pathology Department at the Massachusetts General Hospital  It has not been cleared or approved by the U S  Food and Drug Administration  FDA   The FDA has determined that such clearance or approval is not necessary  this laboratory has established and verified the test s accuracy and precision  Added by  Frederick Carl Koerner  M D  Signed On  01 REMOVED_MONTH 99 at 15 03 GROSS DESCRIPTION  Received fresh   labeled REMOVE_PATIENT_NAME  patient s name  unit number  and  left breast  is a left modified radical mastectomy specimen consisting of a left breast with axillary tissue  30 x 23 x 9cm  a 16 x 12cm overlying skin ellipse with a ill defined  5 5cm well healed scar  and a grossly unremarkable nipple  A 3 x 2 x 2cm focally well circumscribed  round  firm solid tumor is present 8cm inferior to the nipple  0 5cm from the closest inferior inked margin  6cm from the closest deep margin  and 4cm from the closest superior inked margin  The hard  white mass is surrounded by indurated breast tissue with multiple  gritty  focally necrotic areas of breast tissue which extend approximately 3cm from the main tumor mass and extend to within 0 2cm of the superior and inferior margins   The remainder of the breast parenchyma is composed of fibrofatty tissue with more firm  white nodular areas immediately inferior to the skin ellipse  Multiple axillary lymph nodes ranging from 0 5cm to 1 5cm in diameter are identified  SECTION CODE A B  Representative sections of tumor  C  Representative section of tumor in relation to closest inferior inked margin  D G  Representative sections of gritty breast tissue surrounding the main tumor mass  in relation to closest superior and inferior margins  H I  Representative sections of firm  fibrous nodular areas inferior to skin excision  J P  Nodes from axillary tail  Received fresh   labeled REMOVE_PATIENT_NAME  patient s name  unit number  and  right breast  is a 18 x 15 x 4 5cm aggregate of fibrofatty breast tissue  some of which contain overlying tan  wrinkled skin  among which can be identified the nipple areolar area  The fragments of tissue are inked and sectioned to reveal fibrofatty tissue with focal fibrocystic changes  Representative sections of the tissue including overlying skin is submitted in cassettes Q through Z  Received fresh   labeled REMOVE_PATIENT_NAME  patient s name  unit number  and  additional left axillary tissue  is a 1 5cm lymph node  bisected  and submitted entirely in cassette AA  Resident  SAHA SADEGHI  MD                                                                                 SOURCE CARE UNIT  MGH INPATIENT  FLR UNKNOWN  REPORTS TO  Barbara Lynn Smith  M D  Michael J Yaremchuk  M D  W B Jerry Younger  M D  PART A  BREAST  LEFT   MASTECTOMY PART B  SOFT TISSUE  ADDITIONAL LEFT AXILLARY TISSUE   EXC PART C  BREAST  RIGHT   REDUCTION   report_end  , Unchecked.
